1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,399
On the fifth of November, at PM Summit 2026, every breakout room in the session

2
00:00:10,419 --> 00:00:16,957
“PM Role in an AI World” will take on a real project problem with an AI agent.

3
00:00:17,357 --> 00:00:22,729
This briefing explains the situation, what to prepare, and what your room will deliver.

4
00:00:23,529 --> 00:00:26,260
Our role as project managers is changing.

5
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:29,279
AI can already take over much of the routine:

6
00:00:29,299 --> 00:00:32,988
collecting status, chasing updates, writing minutes.

7
00:00:33,238 --> 00:00:37,672
That moves the job from managing work to orchestrating outcomes.

8
00:00:37,692 --> 00:00:39,134
From tasks to impact.

9
00:00:39,154 --> 00:00:42,335
From on time and on budget, to business value.

10
00:00:42,355 --> 00:00:44,214
From reporting to deciding.

11
00:00:44,234 --> 00:00:46,383
From spreadsheets to agents.

12
00:00:47,083 --> 00:00:50,931
What stays with us is what was always the core of the role:

13
00:00:50,951 --> 00:00:54,692
vision, judgment, influence, adaptability, and impact.

14
00:00:54,992 --> 00:00:59,516
So every project manager now needs to define their own work around AI:

15
00:00:59,536 --> 00:01:02,527
what to hand to an agent, and what to keep.

16
00:01:02,927 --> 00:01:04,939
This breakout is where you start.

17
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:07,463
In one hour, with a real project problem,

18
00:01:07,483 --> 00:01:11,931
your room decides what an agent should take, and what stays with you.

19
00:01:13,031 --> 00:01:16,895
Meet KESTREL: a new sensor-hub chip and its evaluation kit,

20
00:01:16,915 --> 00:01:21,724
fourteen days from tape-out, with a lead customer waiting for samples.

21
00:01:22,124 --> 00:01:25,567
Behind it stands a team across design, verification,

22
00:01:25,587 --> 00:01:28,896
test, software, quality, operations and marketing.

23
00:01:28,916 --> 00:01:33,711
Every one of them needs something from the project manager this week.

24
00:01:34,311 --> 00:01:38,035
On Monday, you inherit KESTREL as its new project manager.

25
00:01:38,055 --> 00:01:41,999
By Thursday morning, you are holding six problems at once.

26
00:01:42,399 --> 00:01:47,044
Sofia forwards a customer change request that nobody can answer yet.

27
00:01:47,294 --> 00:01:52,443
Chen mentions a supplier delay, in a risk log no one has touched in five weeks.

28
00:01:52,693 --> 00:01:58,283
Priya flags a late load board, in a meeting where nobody writes down what was decided.

29
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:00,406
Marco’s numbers say one thing.

30
00:02:00,426 --> 00:02:03,340
Last week’s status report still says green.

31
00:02:03,590 --> 00:02:08,468
Aiko’s design is not ready, and steering needs a decision by two o’clock.

32
00:02:08,718 --> 00:02:12,585
And the lessons Jonas left behind arrive three days too late.

33
00:02:13,185 --> 00:02:14,755
None of this is unusual.

34
00:02:14,775 --> 00:02:17,757
It is the week most project managers know.

35
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:21,382
And none of it is the part of the job that matters most:

36
00:02:21,402 --> 00:02:24,270
decisions, stakeholders, and thinking ahead.

37
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:28,766
Before the summit, please complete four preparation steps.

38
00:02:28,786 --> 00:02:32,702
They matter: everything your room does on the day builds on them.

39
00:02:33,052 --> 00:02:33,278
One.

40
00:02:33,298 --> 00:02:37,688
Read “Fourteen Days”, the KESTREL story attached to your invitation.

41
00:02:37,988 --> 00:02:38,248
Two.

42
00:02:38,268 --> 00:02:41,824
Your invitation names its case file, from C1 to C6.

43
00:02:41,844 --> 00:02:46,381
Open it at the back of the story, read it as the project manager,

44
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:50,177
and ask: which parts of this work would I hand off?

45
00:02:50,477 --> 00:02:50,816
Three.

46
00:02:50,836 --> 00:02:51,894
Map your own week.

47
00:02:51,914 --> 00:02:54,710
How long does this task take you, step by step?

48
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:58,643
Mark which steps are repetitive, and which need your judgment.

49
00:02:58,943 --> 00:02:59,220
Four.

50
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:04,922
Bring one idea for what you would do with those hours back: a decision you would prepare better,

51
00:03:04,942 --> 00:03:08,646
a stakeholder you would see sooner, or time to think ahead.

52
00:03:09,146 --> 00:03:12,841
One prerequisite: every room needs at least one member

53
00:03:12,861 --> 00:03:16,364
with Agent Builder activated in Copilot Premium.

54
00:03:16,564 --> 00:03:19,837
If that is you, check your access before the summit.

55
00:03:19,857 --> 00:03:24,301
Everyone else brings ideas and experience, and everyone has a role.

56
00:03:24,451 --> 00:03:26,942
Please don’t bring confidential documents.

57
00:03:26,962 --> 00:03:29,553
Everything you need is in the case file.

58
00:03:30,753 --> 00:03:34,191
On the fifth of November, there is nothing left to prepare.

59
00:03:34,211 --> 00:03:36,403
Each room arrives knowing its job.

60
00:03:36,503 --> 00:03:39,200
In sixty minutes, you map where the hours go,

61
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:44,432
design an agent that takes the repetitive part, and measure the time it gives back.

62
00:03:44,782 --> 00:03:51,357
Your room leaves with a Pilot Pack: the agent’s instructions, the decisions the project manager keeps,

63
00:03:51,377 --> 00:03:58,172
the hours it gives back, one guardrail, an owner, and a first test on a real project after the summit.

64
00:03:59,272 --> 00:04:03,273
At the end, every room meets in the Summit Debrief: hours back,

65
00:04:03,293 --> 00:04:06,847
business impact, the biggest surprise and one guardrail.

66
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:12,173
Then one question decides it: which pilot would you implement on Monday morning?

67
00:04:13,173 --> 00:04:14,846
The agent takes the routine.

68
00:04:14,866 --> 00:04:18,211
You take back the time for the work only you can do.

69
00:04:18,911 --> 00:04:21,582
Read “Fourteen Days” before the fifth of November.

70
00:04:21,602 --> 00:04:23,202
We’ll see you in the room.
